        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do cordless vacuums last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The motor usually outlasts the battery. Owners commonly report that lithium-ion packs lose noticeable capacity after a few years of regular use. With a removable battery and cleaned filters, a good cordless can run for many years, whereas a built-in battery often sets the machine's practical lifespan.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a cordless vacuum as powerful as a corded one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On paper, corded uprights often deliver more sustained suction for the price because they are not limited by a battery. Modern cordless sticks come close for everyday cleaning, especially in boost mode, but they cannot match unlimited runtime. For small and medium homes, the convenience usually outweighs the difference.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I replace the filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Follow the manufacturer's guidance, which for most washable filters means rinsing roughly once a month and replacing every year or so, depending on use. If suction drops or the machine smells musty, check the filter first, and always let it dry completely before refitting.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which cordless vacuum is best for pet hair?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look for an anti-tangle brushroll, a sealed filter and a bin large enough for your home. The Shark Vertex and Dyson V15 Detect are strong candidates, and a small handheld such as the Bissell is useful for furniture. Confirm each model's current specifications before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a cordless vacuum replace a robot vacuum?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They do different jobs. A robot maintains floors day to day while you are out, but it struggles with stairs, edges and deep carpet. A cordless stick handles those jobs. Many homes use both, and our comparison of the two explains how to divide the cleaning between them.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are expensive cordless vacuums worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on your home. Premium sticks add sensing, better filtration, longer-lasting batteries and better build quality, which matter for allergies, pets and heavy use. For a small apartment with mostly hard floors, a mid-range or budget stick often does the job just as well at a lower cost.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a cordless vacuum on wet spills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most cordless stick vacuums are dry-only, and water can damage the motor and battery. If you regularly deal with spills, choose a machine designed for wet and dry use, such as the Roborock Dyad Pro, or use a wet-capable shop vacuum. Check the manual for what your model supports.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

